From: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
Date: Mon, 06 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: intercept a virtual port's uplink

Drive the uplink inject. The previous patch added the primitive that
tags a received frame with a virtual port as its source and pushes it
into the PPE; this hooks the wireless receive path so real packets
reach it, completing the wireless-to-wired offload.

The interception is a netfilter ingress hook on the bound device, not
a netdev_rx_handler. The device a virtual port binds is a wireless VAP
that is a port of the software bridge, and the bridge already owns its
rx_handler; a second rx_handler cannot be registered, and the bind
runs in the flowtable offload work context without the rtnl lock a
handler registration needs. A NETDEV_INGRESS hook runs earlier on the
receive path, coexists with the bridge, is per-device, and registers
under netfilter's own lock. The driver requires CONFIG_NETFILTER,
CONFIG_NETFILTER_INGRESS and CONFIG_INET so every supported build includes
the hook and Internet protocol infrastructure used by this datapath.

The hook injects only IPv4/IPv6 TCP/UDP unicast addressed to the router
whose exact original tuple belongs to a successfully installed hardware
flow for that ingress VP. The flowtable publishes the tuple after making
the hardware row valid and removes it before hardware deletion. New
connections, local traffic and failed offloads therefore remain entirely
on the software path. An unexpected miss atomically disables injection
for the VP before returning its packet to software, so fallback needs no
private skb metadata. Each uplink flow retains its own ingress-VP reference
independently of the reverse destination-VP flow.

Register each per-device ingress hook at the originating flowtable
priority. If another offloaded rule needs the same device at a different
priority, keep that rule in software instead of changing hook ordering.

At the ingress point the receive path has pulled the L2 header and, for a
routed frame crossing a bridged VAP, has not yet reclassified pkt_type.
The decision consequently uses the L2/L3 headers and tuple; L2 is restored
before injection because the PPE parses it to route. The cached bridge MAC
keeps intra-LAN switched traffic on the bridge. A routed VAP without a
bridge master omits that MAC test.

A source-tagged frame whose destination is another virtual port is a
legitimate wireless-to-wireless result and is transmitted out the
destination's bound device. Destination CPU plus source VP is anomalous
after tuple gating. It still takes the VP-specific scatter completion path,
then trips the persistent VP circuit breaker before returning to software.

The hook is registered when possible when a virtual port is first bound
and removed on release, device unregister, or teardown, mirroring the
slot lifecycle. Hook failure does not tear down the independently useful
destination VP. Teardown takes the slot lock so a late release cannot
unregister the same hook twice. Hardware-disable failure poisons the slot
rather than allowing unsafe reuse. This completes the virtual-port uplink;
the whole wireless path - both directions of a routed, NAT'd flow - is
now offloaded.

The VP-to-device mapping is published before its ingress hook goes live,
bridge MAC changes refresh the cached interception address, and removal
quiesces all VP ingress hooks before freeing the physical net devices
borrowed as injection carriers.

The driver now depends on NETFILTER, NETFILTER_INGRESS and INET. The
PPE and Wi-Fi datapaths are part of the same platform integration, so
shipping the driver without its virtual-port ingress path is not a
useful configuration.
Hook registration can still fail at runtime; that failure remains fail-open
and leaves the independently useful destination VP active.

Signed-off-by: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
